  "text": "A framework is introduced to predict the internal forces that arise when layers slide past each other inside a solid crystal. The approach treats the sliding defects as mathematical discontinuities within the theory of elasticity, extending earlier ideas that linked such defects to stress fields. The key assumption is that only the three stress components acting normal to any internal surface must remain continuous across that surface, allowing the model to accommodate defects that cause any kind of jump in displacement. The formulation is expressed in a radial‑angular coordinate system, and general expressions are derived for the stress produced by any prescribed defect. Several illustrative cases are worked out in full, involving both edge‑type and twist‑type defects in round rod‑like bodies. The connection between these mathematical defects and simple slip patterns in crystals is explained, and equivalent groups of defects are identified. By replacing actual slip bands with the appropriate mathematical defects, the remaining elastic region’s stresses can be evaluated using this theory."
